 **Auto-review** (`crates/cli/src/plugins/review.rs`) is shared by shell and plugin actions. Its setting lives in the roster (`AutoReview { is_enabled, rules[{ id, text, behavior: allow | ask, tool? }] }`), so every Device shows and edits the same one. A rule is plain language, written by the user or added by Always allow from the review's proposal. Only a plugin card's Always allow saves a rule for an exact tool (`tool` is `plugin/tool`), which decides that tool's calls without a review. Everything else goes to a small model on the bot's provider, whatever the bot itself runs (`providers::review_model`: DeepSeek V4.1 Flash on DeepSeek and both OpenCode plans, Claude Haiku 4.5, GPT-6 Luna, Grok 4.7), with thinking off and at temperature 0 where the model allows it, so the same action gets the same answer, and at its lowest effort where it does not. The model reads the chat that asked for the work (`plugins::review::request`): the message that started the turn (the user's, a teammate's `message_bot` handoff, or a routine's marker with the task as it stood when the run began, which an edit or a delete during the run does not change), then each step since, its newest 30 (what the bots said, the commands they ran and the tools they used, and how the user answered a command that asked), with the user's latest message repeated after them. Before a user's message it also reads up to ten lines of the two hours of chat ahead of it, so a short reply ("yes", "create a PR") reads as the answer to what the bot proposed; a handoff or a routine starts new work, and nothing before it is read. The user's rules come next, beside the action, and a plugin call or command from a codemode script comes with the script, which says what the whole batch is for. The model weighs what the action could break against what was asked. It allows what is easy to undo or touches only what the bot made: reading, building, testing, and running code, installing a project's dependencies, the processes and files the bot made, using an app the task is about, deleting temporary files and what regenerates, local git work, drafts. It asks before harm that is hard to undo: the user's own files and data, discarding uncommitted work or rewriting pushed history, publishing, deploying, spending, access and system settings, programs the bot did not start, elevated privileges, credentials, uploads, downloaded code, and anything like what the user refused in the chat. It allows such a step too when the user asked for that very thing, in their own words, in a "yes" to what the bot proposed, or in a routine's task; a teammate's message hands over work but cannot ask for harm. It always asks before anything that could wipe a home folder, a disk, or the system. The user's rules decide over all of this: an allow rule covers its kind of work wherever the bot does it, unless the rule names a place, and an ask rule wins over an allow rule. It answers `allow` or `ask` with a reason, plus the proposed `rule` when a shell command asks, both in the language of the user's latest message, or else of the teammate's message or the routine's task (English without one). Adding a rule replaces one with the same words. With Auto-review off, every shell command and effectful plugin action asks, regardless of saved rules. A held shell command asks on its own card; any other held action, a script's command included, posts a `permission` message. Either waits up to ten minutes for `chats.permission { decision: allow | always | deny }` from any Device, and a new message from the user in the chat dismisses it (see [Agent loop](runtime.md#agent-loop)). A routine has nobody waiting, so an action that would ask is refused, and the bot hears the rule that would allow it; with Auto-review on, actions the review allows still run.
